 ],
 "literal": "Pitiable indeed are they, the small white water-lilies! / When we were young, they became foliage garments; but now, / since the husband of great wealth died, restricting the times of meals, / during these painful days, the eating / lily-derived meager food they have become.",
 "translation": "How pitiable they are, those small white water-lilies! When we were young, they served as our beautiful foliage garments. But now, since my husband of great wealth has passed away, they have become the meager food gathered from lily seeds, eaten only at restricted times during these miserable days of widowhood.",
 "modern": "சிறிய வெண்மையான ஆம்பல் மலர்கள் மிகவும் இரங்கத்தக்கவை. நாம் இளமையாக இருந்த காலத்தில் அவை நமக்கு ஆடையாகவோ அணிகலனாகவோ பயன்பட்டன. ஆனால் இப்பொழுது, மிகுந்த செல்வத்தை உடைய கணவன் இறந்துவிட்டதால், பொழுதுகளை மறுத்து, துன்பம் தரும் இந்நாட்களில் உண்ணும் அல்லி மலரிலிருந்து பெறப்படும் எளிய உணவாக அவை மாறிவிட்டன.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by a grieving widow who reflects on her drastically changed life after her husband's death. In her youth, the small white water-lilies were used joyfully as decorative foliage garments during happy times with her husband. Now, in her state of ascetic widowhood (tāpatanilai), those same lilies have become her meager, restricted diet. She must skip regular meals and eat only the simple lily seeds or petals to survive these painful days. The contrast between the lilies as ornaments of youth and lilies as the food of sorrow highlights the harsh reality of her loss. This poignant transition underscores the theme of impermanence and the austere life forced upon widows in ancient society.",
